---
title: Polar and Radar Charts
description: Build native pie, donut, gauge, radar, line, scatter, radial bar, rose, and sunburst charts through the opt-in polar coordinate entry.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Polar geometry is available only from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/polar"},{"type":"text","value":". The container\nowns responsive center, angle, and radius ranges. Its eager "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pie"},{"type":"text","value":" transform\nowns value allocation; granular D3 modules still own configured scales, curve\nfactories, and final arc/path geometry."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  angleGrid,\n  pie,\n  polar,\n  radialArc,\n  radialArea,\n  radialDot,\n  radialGrid,\n  radialLine,\n  radialRule,\n  radialText,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/polar'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The package root stays Cartesian-sized when this subpath is not imported."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"pie-and-donut","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pie and donut"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pie"},{"type":"text","value":" to turn totals into flat source-linked angular intervals.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialArc"},{"type":"text","value":" renders the intervals. This example uses a responsive inner radius\nfor a donut; return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":" instead for a pie."}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"polar-pie-donut-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"polar-pie-donut","data-collapsed-indexes":"[1]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { pie, polar, radialArc } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\\nimport { alphabet } from './data'\\n\\nconst slices = pie(alphabet, { value: 'frequency' })\\nconst letters = alphabet.map((row) => row.letter)\\n\\nexport default defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    polar({\\n      inset: 8,\\n      radiusRatio: 0.82,\\n      marks: [\\n        radialArc(slices, {\\n          innerRadius: ({ radius }) => radius * 0.58,\\n          cornerRadius: 4,\\n          color: 'letter',\\n          key: 'letter',\\n        }),\\n      ],\\n      scales: {\\n        angle: null,\\n        radius: null,\\n      },\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: null,\\n    y: null,\\n  },\\n  color: {\\n    domain: letters,\\n    range: ['#0ea5e9', '#6366f1', '#a855f7', '#ec4899', '#f97316', '#94a3b8'],\\n  },\\n})\",\"/src/data.ts\":\"export interface AlphabetRow {\\n  letter: string\\n  frequency: number\\n}\\n\\nexport const alphabet: readonly AlphabetRow[] = [\\n  { letter: 'E', frequency: 0.12702 },\\n  { letter: 'T', frequency: 0.09056 },\\n  { letter: 'A', frequency: 0.08167 },\\n  { letter: 'O', frequency: 0.07507 },\\n  { letter: 'I', frequency: 0.06966 },\\n  { letter: 'Other', frequency: 0.55602 },\\n]\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { pie, polar, radialArc } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { alphabet } from './data'\n\nconst slices = pie(alphabet, { value: 'frequency' })\nconst letters = alphabet.map((row) => row.letter)\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      inset: 8,\n      radiusRatio: 0.82,\n      marks: [\n        radialArc(slices, {\n          innerRadius: ({ radius }) => radius * 0.58,\n          cornerRadius: 4,\n          color: 'letter',\n          key: 'letter',\n        }),\n      ],\n      scales: {\n        angle: null,\n        radius: null,\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  color: {\n    domain: letters,\n    range: ['#0ea5e9', '#6366f1', '#a855f7', '#ec4899', '#f97316', '#94a3b8'],\n  },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=polar-pie-donut env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"},{"type":"code","value":"export interface AlphabetRow {\n  letter: string\n  frequency: number\n}\n\nexport const alphabet: readonly AlphabetRow[] = [\n  { letter: 'E', frequency: 0.12702 },\n  { letter: 'T', frequency: 0.09056 },\n  { letter: 'A', frequency: 0.08167 },\n  { letter: 'O', frequency: 0.07507 },\n  { letter: 'I', frequency: 0.06966 },\n  { letter: 'Other', frequency: 0.55602 },\n]","lang":"ts","meta":"group=polar-pie-donut file=/src/data.ts collapsed","title":"/src/data.ts","file":"/src/data.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same primitives cover labels, center content, padding, rounded corners,\nand concentric rings. See the catalog examples for a\n"},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/93-labeled-pie/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"labeled pie"}]},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/94-center-donut/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"center-content donut"}]},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/95-rounded-donut/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"rounded donut"}]},{"type":"text","value":", and\n"},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/96-nested-donut/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"nested donut"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Radial offsets are signed pixels applied after scale mapping. They do not\nchange the radius domain or reserve outer margin; leave space with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radiusRatio"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inset"},{"type":"text","value":", or chart margins."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source order is the default. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"orderBy"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"order"},{"type":"text","value":" only for an explicit\nangular sort. Stable arc keys must come from the original row, not the\ngenerated slice index."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each allocated row keeps the original fields plus direct "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sourceIndexes"},{"type":"text","value":" lineage. Fixed allocation fields overwrite source fields with\nthe same names. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gapAngle"},{"type":"text","value":" materializes direct empty space; the returned\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"padAngle: 0"},{"type":"text","value":" prevents "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialArc"},{"type":"text","value":" from padding that interval again."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"partial-circle-gauge","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Partial-circle gauge"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A gauge is the same composition over a restricted pie interval. It is not a\nseparate geometry implementation."}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"polar-partial-gauge-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"polar-partial-gauge","data-collapsed-indexes":"[]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { pie, polar, radialArc, radialText } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\\n\\nconst value = Math.max(0, Math.min(100, 72))\\nconst reading = { id: 'complete', value } as const\\nconst parts = [reading, { id: 'remaining', value: 100 - value }] as const\\nconst slices = pie(parts, {\\n  value: 'value',\\n  startAngle: -Math.PI * 0.75,\\n  endAngle: Math.PI * 0.75,\\n})\\n\\nexport default defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    polar({\\n      radiusRatio: 0.84,\\n      scales: {\\n        angle: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 1]) },\\n        radius: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 1]) },\\n      },\\n\\n      marks: [\\n        radialArc(slices, {\\n          innerRadius: ({ radius }) => radius * 0.72,\\n          cornerRadius: 999,\\n          color: 'id',\\n          key: 'id',\\n        }),\\n        radialText([reading], {\\n          angle: 0,\\n          radius: 0,\\n          text: (row) => `${row.value}%`,\\n          key: 'id',\\n          fill: 'currentColor',\\n          fontSize: 20,\\n          fontWeight: 700,\\n        }),\\n      ],\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: null,\\n    y: null,\\n  },\\n  color: {\\n    domain: ['complete', 'remaining'],\\n    range: ['#ef4444', '#e2e8f0'],\\n  },\\n})\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { pie, polar, radialArc, radialText } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst value = Math.max(0, Math.min(100, 72))\nconst reading = { id: 'complete', value } as const\nconst parts = [reading, { id: 'remaining', value: 100 - value }] as const\nconst slices = pie(parts, {\n  value: 'value',\n  startAngle: -Math.PI * 0.75,\n  endAngle: Math.PI * 0.75,\n})\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      radiusRatio: 0.84,\n      scales: {\n        angle: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 1]) },\n        radius: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 1]) },\n      },\n\n      marks: [\n        radialArc(slices, {\n          innerRadius: ({ radius }) => radius * 0.72,\n          cornerRadius: 999,\n          color: 'id',\n          key: 'id',\n        }),\n        radialText([reading], {\n          angle: 0,\n          radius: 0,\n          text: (row) => `${row.value}%`,\n          key: 'id',\n          fill: 'currentColor',\n          fontSize: 20,\n          fontWeight: 700,\n        }),\n      ],\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  color: {\n    domain: ['complete', 'remaining'],\n    range: ['#ef4444', '#e2e8f0'],\n  },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=polar-partial-gauge env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bound the input before layout and expose the exact value outside the arc. Arc\nlength is useful for a compact status summary, not fine comparison. Add ticks,\na needle, and a center label only when they carry meaning; see the\n"},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/98-needle-gauge/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"needle gauge"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for that\ncomposition."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"radar-profile","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Radar profile"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Radar combines an inferred angle factory and a fixed radius instance with\npolar guides and radial marks. TanStack supplies both responsive ranges. The\nnormalization remains visible in a separate source file because it determines\nthe meaning of every radius."}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"polar-radar-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"polar-radar","data-collapsed-indexes":"[1,2]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport {\\n  angleGrid,\\n  polar,\\n  radialArea,\\n  radialGrid,\\n  radialLine,\\n} from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\\nimport { curveLinearClosed } from 'd3-shape'\\nimport { events } from './data'\\nimport { profile } from './normalize'\\n\\nexport default defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    polar({\\n      radiusRatio: 0.72,\\n      scales: {\\n        angle: { scale: scalePoint<string>().domain(events), wrap: true },\\n        radius: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 1]) },\\n      },\\n\\n      guides: [\\n        radialGrid({\\n          values: [0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1],\\n          shape: 'polygon',\\n        }),\\n        angleGrid({ labels: true }),\\n      ],\\n      marks: [\\n        radialArea(profile, {\\n          angle: 'event',\\n          radius: 'relativePerformance',\\n          curve: curveLinearClosed,\\n          fill: '#7c3aed',\\n          fillOpacity: 0.22,\\n        }),\\n        radialLine(profile, {\\n          angle: 'event',\\n          radius: 'relativePerformance',\\n          curve: curveLinearClosed,\\n          stroke: '#8b5cf6',\\n          strokeWidth: 2,\\n        }),\\n      ],\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: null,\\n    y: null,\\n  },\\n})\",\"/src/data.ts\":\"export interface DecathlonRow {\\n  Country: string\\n  '100 Meters': number\\n  'Long Jump': number\\n  'High Jump': number\\n  '100 Meter Hurdles': number\\n}\\n\\nexport const decathlon: readonly DecathlonRow[] = [\\n  {\\n    Country: 'United States',\\n    '100 Meters': 10.35,\\n    'Long Jump': 7.96,\\n    'High Jump': 2.05,\\n    '100 Meter Hurdles': 13.61,\\n  },\\n  {\\n    Country: 'Great Britain',\\n    '100 Meters': 10.44,\\n    'Long Jump': 7.74,\\n    'High Jump': 2.11,\\n    '100 Meter Hurdles': 13.75,\\n  },\\n  {\\n    Country: 'Germany',\\n    '100 Meters': 10.67,\\n    'Long Jump': 7.62,\\n    'High Jump': 2.08,\\n    '100 Meter Hurdles': 14.02,\\n  },\\n  {\\n    Country: 'France',\\n    '100 Meters': 10.58,\\n    'Long Jump': 7.81,\\n    'High Jump': 1.99,\\n    '100 Meter Hurdles': 13.88,\\n  },\\n]\\n\\nexport const events = [\\n  '100 Meters',\\n  'Long Jump',\\n  'High Jump',\\n  '100 Meter Hurdles',\\n] as const\\n\\nexport type RadarEvent = (typeof events)[number]\",\"/src/normalize.ts\":\"import { normalize, select } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { fold } from '@tanstack/charts/transform/fold'\\nimport { decathlon, events } from './data'\\nimport type { RadarEvent } from './data'\\n\\nconst timedEvents = new Set<RadarEvent>(['100 Meters', '100 Meter Hurdles'])\\nconst folded = fold(decathlon, {\\n  fields: events,\\n  as: { key: 'event', value: 'result' },\\n})\\nconst normalized = normalize(folded, {\\n  by: 'event',\\n  value: (datum) =>\\n    timedEvents.has(datum.event) ? -datum.result : datum.result,\\n  basis: 'extent',\\n  as: 'relativePerformance',\\n})\\n\\nexport const profile = select(normalized, {\\n  by: 'event',\\n  select: 'first',\\n})\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport {\n  angleGrid,\n  polar,\n  radialArea,\n  radialGrid,\n  radialLine,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\nimport { curveLinearClosed } from 'd3-shape'\nimport { events } from './data'\nimport { profile } from './normalize'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      radiusRatio: 0.72,\n      scales: {\n        angle: { scale: scalePoint<string>().domain(events), wrap: true },\n        radius: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 1]) },\n      },\n\n      guides: [\n        radialGrid({\n          values: [0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1],\n          shape: 'polygon',\n        }),\n        angleGrid({ labels: true }),\n      ],\n      marks: [\n        radialArea(profile, {\n          angle: 'event',\n          radius: 'relativePerformance',\n          curve: curveLinearClosed,\n          fill: '#7c3aed',\n          fillOpacity: 0.22,\n        }),\n        radialLine(profile, {\n          angle: 'event',\n          radius: 'relativePerformance',\n          curve: curveLinearClosed,\n          stroke: '#8b5cf6',\n          strokeWidth: 2,\n        }),\n      ],\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=polar-radar env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"},{"type":"code","value":"export interface DecathlonRow {\n  Country: string\n  '100 Meters': number\n  'Long Jump': number\n  'High Jump': number\n  '100 Meter Hurdles': number\n}\n\nexport const decathlon: readonly DecathlonRow[] = [\n  {\n    Country: 'United States',\n    '100 Meters': 10.35,\n    'Long Jump': 7.96,\n    'High Jump': 2.05,\n    '100 Meter Hurdles': 13.61,\n  },\n  {\n    Country: 'Great Britain',\n    '100 Meters': 10.44,\n    'Long Jump': 7.74,\n    'High Jump': 2.11,\n    '100 Meter Hurdles': 13.75,\n  },\n  {\n    Country: 'Germany',\n    '100 Meters': 10.67,\n    'Long Jump': 7.62,\n    'High Jump': 2.08,\n    '100 Meter Hurdles': 14.02,\n  },\n  {\n    Country: 'France',\n    '100 Meters': 10.58,\n    'Long Jump': 7.81,\n    'High Jump': 1.99,\n    '100 Meter Hurdles': 13.88,\n  },\n]\n\nexport const events = [\n  '100 Meters',\n  'Long Jump',\n  'High Jump',\n  '100 Meter Hurdles',\n] as const\n\nexport type RadarEvent = (typeof events)[number]","lang":"ts","meta":"group=polar-radar file=/src/data.ts collapsed","title":"/src/data.ts","file":"/src/data.ts"},{"type":"code","value":"import { normalize, select } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { fold } from '@tanstack/charts/transform/fold'\nimport { decathlon, events } from './data'\nimport type { RadarEvent } from './data'\n\nconst timedEvents = new Set<RadarEvent>(['100 Meters', '100 Meter Hurdles'])\nconst folded = fold(decathlon, {\n  fields: events,\n  as: { key: 'event', value: 'result' },\n})\nconst normalized = normalize(folded, {\n  by: 'event',\n  value: (datum) =>\n    timedEvents.has(datum.event) ? -datum.result : datum.result,\n  basis: 'extent',\n  as: 'relativePerformance',\n})\n\nexport const profile = select(normalized, {\n  by: 'event',\n  select: 'first',\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=polar-radar file=/src/normalize.ts collapsed","title":"/src/normalize.ts","file":"/src/normalize.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use radar for a small, fixed set of compatible dimensions. Keep every domain\nand direction explicit, and do not rank profiles by apparent filled area. See\nthe "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/99-comparative-radar/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"comparative radar"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nwhen multiple profiles are the point of the chart."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"numeric-polar-line","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Numeric polar line"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lightweight linear scales map numeric angle and radius values without changing\nthe mark API. Here a visible transform maps observation dates to angles while\npreserving the source temperature field."}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"polar-line-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"polar-line","data-collapsed-indexes":"[1]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport {\\n  angleGrid,\\n  polar,\\n  radialGrid,\\n  radialLine,\\n} from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\\nimport { dayOfYearAngle, seattle2012 } from './weather'\\n\\nexport default defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    polar({\\n      scales: {\\n        angle: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 360]) },\\n        radius: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([-10, 40]) },\\n      },\\n\\n      guides: [\\n        radialGrid({ values: [0, 10, 20, 30, 40] }),\\n        angleGrid({ values: [0, 90, 180, 270], labels: false }),\\n      ],\\n      marks: [\\n        radialLine(seattle2012, {\\n          angle: dayOfYearAngle,\\n          radius: 'temp_max',\\n          stroke: '#0f766e',\\n        }),\\n      ],\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: null,\\n    y: null,\\n  },\\n})\",\"/src/weather.ts\":\"export interface WeatherRow {\\n  location: string\\n  date: Date\\n  temp_max: number\\n}\\n\\nconst weather: readonly WeatherRow[] = [\\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-01-15'), temp_max: 8.3 },\\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-03-15'), temp_max: 12.2 },\\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-05-15'), temp_max: 18.9 },\\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-07-15'), temp_max: 25.6 },\\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-09-15'), temp_max: 21.1 },\\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-11-15'), temp_max: 11.7 },\\n]\\n\\nexport const seattle2012 = weather.filter(\\n  (row) => row.location === 'Seattle' && row.date.getUTCFullYear() === 2012,\\n)\\n\\nexport function dayOfYearAngle(row: WeatherRow) {\\n  const year = row.date.getUTCFullYear()\\n  const start = Date.UTC(year, 0, 1)\\n  const end = Date.UTC(year + 1, 0, 1)\\n  return ((row.date.getTime() - start) / (end - start)) * 360\\n}\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport {\n  angleGrid,\n  polar,\n  radialGrid,\n  radialLine,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { dayOfYearAngle, seattle2012 } from './weather'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      scales: {\n        angle: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 360]) },\n        radius: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([-10, 40]) },\n      },\n\n      guides: [\n        radialGrid({ values: [0, 10, 20, 30, 40] }),\n        angleGrid({ values: [0, 90, 180, 270], labels: false }),\n      ],\n      marks: [\n        radialLine(seattle2012, {\n          angle: dayOfYearAngle,\n          radius: 'temp_max',\n          stroke: '#0f766e',\n        }),\n      ],\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=polar-line env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"},{"type":"code","value":"export interface WeatherRow {\n  location: string\n  date: Date\n  temp_max: number\n}\n\nconst weather: readonly WeatherRow[] = [\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-01-15'), temp_max: 8.3 },\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-03-15'), temp_max: 12.2 },\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-05-15'), temp_max: 18.9 },\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-07-15'), temp_max: 25.6 },\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-09-15'), temp_max: 21.1 },\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-11-15'), temp_max: 11.7 },\n]\n\nexport const seattle2012 = weather.filter(\n  (row) => row.location === 'Seattle' && row.date.getUTCFullYear() === 2012,\n)\n\nexport function dayOfYearAngle(row: WeatherRow) {\n  const year = row.date.getUTCFullYear()\n  const start = Date.UTC(year, 0, 1)\n  const end = Date.UTC(year + 1, 0, 1)\n  return ((row.date.getTime() - start) / (end - start)) * 360\n}","lang":"ts","meta":"group=polar-line file=/src/weather.ts collapsed","title":"/src/weather.ts","file":"/src/weather.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/106-polar-line/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"full-year polar line"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nuses the same transform with the complete weather series."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"numeric-polar-scatter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Numeric polar scatter"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same coordinate accepts independent points. Wind direction and speed stay\nas explicit transforms over source "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"u"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"v"},{"type":"text","value":" measurements."}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"polar-scatter-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"polar-scatter","data-collapsed-indexes":"[1]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { angleGrid, polar, radialDot, radialGrid } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\\nimport { latitudeBand, windDirection, windSpeed } from './wind'\\n\\nexport default defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    polar({\\n      scales: {\\n        angle: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 360]) },\\n        radius: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 13]) },\\n      },\\n\\n      guides: [\\n        radialGrid({ values: [3, 6, 9, 12] }),\\n        angleGrid({ values: [0, 90, 180, 270], labels: false }),\\n      ],\\n      marks: [\\n        radialDot(latitudeBand, {\\n          angle: windDirection,\\n          radius: windSpeed,\\n          r: 4.5,\\n          fill: '#e11d48',\\n        }),\\n      ],\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: null,\\n    y: null,\\n  },\\n})\",\"/src/wind.ts\":\"export interface WindRow {\\n  latitude: number\\n  u: number\\n  v: number\\n}\\n\\nconst wind: readonly WindRow[] = [\\n  { latitude: 48.125, u: 4.2, v: 1.6 },\\n  { latitude: 48.125, u: 2.1, v: 5.8 },\\n  { latitude: 48.125, u: -3.4, v: 6.2 },\\n  { latitude: 48.125, u: -5.1, v: -2.3 },\\n  { latitude: 48.125, u: 1.8, v: -4.7 },\\n]\\n\\nexport const latitudeBand = wind.filter((row) => row.latitude === 48.125)\\n\\nexport function windDirection(row: WindRow) {\\n  return (Math.atan2(row.v, row.u) * (180 / Math.PI) + 360) % 360\\n}\\n\\nexport function windSpeed(row: WindRow) {\\n  return Math.hypot(row.u, row.v)\\n}\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { angleGrid, polar, radialDot, radialGrid } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { latitudeBand, windDirection, windSpeed } from './wind'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      scales: {\n        angle: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 360]) },\n        radius: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 13]) },\n      },\n\n      guides: [\n        radialGrid({ values: [3, 6, 9, 12] }),\n        angleGrid({ values: [0, 90, 180, 270], labels: false }),\n      ],\n      marks: [\n        radialDot(latitudeBand, {\n          angle: windDirection,\n          radius: windSpeed,\n          r: 4.5,\n          fill: '#e11d48',\n        }),\n      ],\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=polar-scatter env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"},{"type":"code","value":"export interface WindRow {\n  latitude: number\n  u: number\n  v: number\n}\n\nconst wind: readonly WindRow[] = [\n  { latitude: 48.125, u: 4.2, v: 1.6 },\n  { latitude: 48.125, u: 2.1, v: 5.8 },\n  { latitude: 48.125, u: -3.4, v: 6.2 },\n  { latitude: 48.125, u: -5.1, v: -2.3 },\n  { latitude: 48.125, u: 1.8, v: -4.7 },\n]\n\nexport const latitudeBand = wind.filter((row) => row.latitude === 48.125)\n\nexport function windDirection(row: WindRow) {\n  return (Math.atan2(row.v, row.u) * (180 / Math.PI) + 360) % 360\n}\n\nexport function windSpeed(row: WindRow) {\n  return Math.hypot(row.u, row.v)\n}","lang":"ts","meta":"group=polar-scatter file=/src/wind.ts collapsed","title":"/src/wind.ts","file":"/src/wind.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/107-polar-scatter/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"catalog polar scatter"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nuses a denser sample from the same latitude band."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"radial-bars","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Radial bars"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose the mark by the quantitative direction. A rose extends one bar through\nradius for each angle band. Concentric radial bars extend through angle for\neach radius band. Band padding controls categorical occupancy."}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"polar-radial-bars-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"polar-radial-bars","data-collapsed-indexes":"[1]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { polar, radialBarRadius } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\\nimport { frequencies } from './data'\\n\\nconst letters = frequencies.map((row) => row.letter)\\nconst maximum = Math.max(...frequencies.map((row) => row.frequency))\\n\\nexport default defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    polar({\\n      radiusRatio: 0.8,\\n      scales: {\\n        angle: { scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.12) },\\n        radius: {\\n          scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, maximum]),\\n          range: [({ radius }) => radius * 0.3, ({ radius }) => radius],\\n        },\\n      },\\n\\n      marks: [\\n        radialBarRadius(frequencies, {\\n          angle: 'letter',\\n          radius: 'frequency',\\n          color: 'letter',\\n          key: 'letter',\\n        }),\\n      ],\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: null,\\n    y: null,\\n  },\\n  color: {\\n    domain: letters,\\n    range: ['#2563eb', '#7c3aed', '#db2777', '#ea580c', '#16a34a'],\\n  },\\n})\",\"/src/data.ts\":\"export interface FrequencyRow {\\n  letter: string\\n  frequency: number\\n}\\n\\nexport const frequencies: readonly FrequencyRow[] = [\\n  { letter: 'E', frequency: 0.12702 },\\n  { letter: 'T', frequency: 0.09056 },\\n  { letter: 'A', frequency: 0.08167 },\\n  { letter: 'O', frequency: 0.07507 },\\n  { letter: 'I', frequency: 0.06966 },\\n]\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { polar, radialBarRadius } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { frequencies } from './data'\n\nconst letters = frequencies.map((row) => row.letter)\nconst maximum = Math.max(...frequencies.map((row) => row.frequency))\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      radiusRatio: 0.8,\n      scales: {\n        angle: { scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.12) },\n        radius: {\n          scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, maximum]),\n          range: [({ radius }) => radius * 0.3, ({ radius }) => radius],\n        },\n      },\n\n      marks: [\n        radialBarRadius(frequencies, {\n          angle: 'letter',\n          radius: 'frequency',\n          color: 'letter',\n          key: 'letter',\n        }),\n      ],\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  color: {\n    domain: letters,\n    range: ['#2563eb', '#7c3aed', '#db2777', '#ea580c', '#16a34a'],\n  },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=polar-radial-bars env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"},{"type":"code","value":"export interface FrequencyRow {\n  letter: string\n  frequency: number\n}\n\nexport const frequencies: readonly FrequencyRow[] = [\n  { letter: 'E', frequency: 0.12702 },\n  { letter: 'T', frequency: 0.09056 },\n  { letter: 'A', frequency: 0.08167 },\n  { letter: 'O', frequency: 0.07507 },\n  { letter: 'I', frequency: 0.06966 },\n]","lang":"ts","meta":"group=polar-radial-bars file=/src/data.ts collapsed","title":"/src/data.ts","file":"/src/data.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An omitted radius baseline in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialBarRadius"},{"type":"text","value":" starts at the physical center;\nthe responsive radius range controls the quantitative endpoints. Supply\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius1"},{"type":"text","value":" when both endpoints are semantic values. Signed radius data should\nuse "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radius1: 0"},{"type":"text","value":" so semantic zero maps through the scale."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialBarAngle"},{"type":"text","value":" when values should extend around the circle instead. See\nthe "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/100-radial-bars/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"concentric radial-bar example"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"polar-hierarchy","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Polar hierarchy"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sunburst"},{"type":"text","value":" mark accepts flat hierarchy rows and owns value\naggregation, partitioning, responsive rings, and sector geometry."}]},{"type":"component","name":"live-example","tagName":"md-live-example","attributes":{"id":"polar-sunburst-1"},"properties":{"data-example-group":"polar-sunburst","data-collapsed-indexes":"[1]","data-workspace":"{\"version\":1,\"entry\":\"/src/chart.ts\",\"environment\":\"charts\",\"files\":{\"/src/chart.ts\":\"import { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\\nimport { sunburst } from '@tanstack/charts/hierarchy/sunburst'\\nimport { polar } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\\nimport { rows } from './data'\\n\\nexport default defineChart({\\n  marks: [\\n    polar({\\n      radiusRatio: 0.88,\\n      startAngle: Math.PI / 2,\\n      endAngle: Math.PI / 2 - Math.PI * 2,\\n      marks: [\\n        sunburst(rows, {\\n          path: 'name',\\n          delimiter: '.',\\n          value: 'size',\\n          innerRadius: ({ radius }) => radius * 0.14,\\n          ringPadding: 2,\\n          color: 'branchId',\\n          stroke: '#fff',\\n        }),\\n      ],\\n      scales: {\\n        angle: null,\\n        radius: null,\\n      },\\n    }),\\n  ],\\n  scales: {\\n    x: null,\\n    y: null,\\n  },\\n  color: { range: ['#7c3aed', '#0ea5e9', '#14b8a6'] },\\n})\",\"/src/data.ts\":\"export interface PackageRow {\\n  name: string\\n  size: number | null\\n}\\n\\nexport const rows: readonly PackageRow[] = [\\n  { name: 'app', size: null },\\n  { name: 'app.ui', size: null },\\n  { name: 'app.ui.button', size: 8 },\\n  { name: 'app.ui.dialog', size: 5 },\\n  { name: 'app.data', size: null },\\n  { name: 'app.data.cache', size: 6 },\\n  { name: 'app.data.client', size: 11 },\\n]\"}}"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"import { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { sunburst } from '@tanstack/charts/hierarchy/sunburst'\nimport { polar } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { rows } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      radiusRatio: 0.88,\n      startAngle: Math.PI / 2,\n      endAngle: Math.PI / 2 - Math.PI * 2,\n      marks: [\n        sunburst(rows, {\n          path: 'name',\n          delimiter: '.',\n          value: 'size',\n          innerRadius: ({ radius }) => radius * 0.14,\n          ringPadding: 2,\n          color: 'branchId',\n          stroke: '#fff',\n        }),\n      ],\n      scales: {\n        angle: null,\n        radius: null,\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  color: { range: ['#7c3aed', '#0ea5e9', '#14b8a6'] },\n})","lang":"ts","meta":"group=polar-sunburst env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry","title":"/src/chart.ts","file":"/src/chart.ts"},{"type":"code","value":"export interface PackageRow {\n  name: string\n  size: number | null\n}\n\nexport const rows: readonly PackageRow[] = [\n  { name: 'app', size: null },\n  { name: 'app.ui', size: null },\n  { name: 'app.ui.button', size: 8 },\n  { name: 'app.ui.dialog', size: 5 },\n  { name: 'app.data', size: null },\n  { name: 'app.data.cache', size: 6 },\n  { name: 'app.data.client', size: 11 },\n]","lang":"ts","meta":"group=polar-sunburst file=/src/data.ts collapsed","title":"/src/data.ts","file":"/src/data.ts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nodeId"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parentId"},{"type":"text","value":" for explicit parent-reference rows. Responsive\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"innerRadius"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"outerRadius"},{"type":"text","value":" callbacks receive the final polar radius;\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ringPadding"},{"type":"text","value":" remains a fixed pixel gap. Every "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SunburstNode"},{"type":"text","value":" retains its\ndirect row and source index, while "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"branchId"},{"type":"text","value":" gives descendants the color of\ntheir first ancestor below the root. See the\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/sunburst.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sunburst Mark reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and the\n"},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/101-sunburst/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"full Flare hierarchy"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"coordinate-and-bundle-boundary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Coordinate and bundle boundary"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"polar()"},{"type":"text","value":" is a positionless container mark. It resolves one center and radius,\ncopies configured angle/radius scales, paints guide backgrounds, child marks,\nthen guide foreground labels, and emits ordinary scene nodes and focus points.\nThe outer chart therefore omits both Cartesian axes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The polar entry uses D3 arc and radial path generators internally. Application\nsource can use compact angle and radius scales or upgrade either one to\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-scale"},{"type":"text","value":"; curve factories and application-owned pie layout can come directly\nfrom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3-shape"},{"type":"text","value":". See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/marks/polar.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Polar Marks"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the complete API and\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/bundle-size-and-performance.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Bundle Size and Performance"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for\nthe isolated consumer budgets."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"radialArc"},{"type":"text","value":" also accepts existing D3 pie DTOs as interoperability input; native\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pie"},{"type":"text","value":" is preferred when flat fields, transform lineage, and direct gap\nsemantics are wanted."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"production-checks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production checks"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep angle for cyclic order or part-to-whole intervals."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use native "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pie"},{"type":"text","value":" output rather than reimplementing angle accumulation."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let marks infer identity from source IDs or unique positions; supply a key\nwhen neither is available."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preserve original values for tooltips and accessible summaries."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep radar dimension domains, directions, and units explicit."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Verify labels around the full circumference at narrow widths."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prefer aligned bars or dots when precise comparison is the primary task."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"pie-and-donut","text":"Pie and donut","level":2},{"id":"partial-circle-gauge","text":"Partial-circle gauge","level":2},{"id":"radar-profile","text":"Radar profile","level":2},{"id":"numeric-polar-line","text":"Numeric polar line","level":2},{"id":"numeric-polar-scatter","text":"Numeric polar scatter","level":2},{"id":"radial-bars","text":"Radial bars","level":2},{"id":"polar-hierarchy","text":"Polar hierarchy","level":2},{"id":"coordinate-and-bundle-boundary","text":"Coordinate and bundle boundary","level":2},{"id":"production-checks","text":"Production checks","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nPolar geometry is available only from `@tanstack/charts/polar`. The container\nowns responsive center, angle, and radius ranges. Its eager `pie` transform\nowns value allocation; granular D3 modules still own configured scales, curve\nfactories, and final arc/path geometry.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  angleGrid,\n  pie,\n  polar,\n  radialArc,\n  radialArea,\n  radialDot,\n  radialGrid,\n  radialLine,\n  radialRule,\n  radialText,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\n```\n\nThe package root stays Cartesian-sized when this subpath is not imported.\n\n## Pie and donut\n\nUse `pie` to turn totals into flat source-linked angular intervals.\n`radialArc` renders the intervals. This example uses a responsive inner radius\nfor a donut; return `0` instead for a pie.\n\n```ts group=polar-pie-donut env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { pie, polar, radialArc } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { alphabet } from './data'\n\nconst slices = pie(alphabet, { value: 'frequency' })\nconst letters = alphabet.map((row) => row.letter)\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      inset: 8,\n      radiusRatio: 0.82,\n      marks: [\n        radialArc(slices, {\n          innerRadius: ({ radius }) => radius * 0.58,\n          cornerRadius: 4,\n          color: 'letter',\n          key: 'letter',\n        }),\n      ],\n      scales: {\n        angle: null,\n        radius: null,\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  color: {\n    domain: letters,\n    range: ['#0ea5e9', '#6366f1', '#a855f7', '#ec4899', '#f97316', '#94a3b8'],\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=polar-pie-donut file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport interface AlphabetRow {\n  letter: string\n  frequency: number\n}\n\nexport const alphabet: readonly AlphabetRow[] = [\n  { letter: 'E', frequency: 0.12702 },\n  { letter: 'T', frequency: 0.09056 },\n  { letter: 'A', frequency: 0.08167 },\n  { letter: 'O', frequency: 0.07507 },\n  { letter: 'I', frequency: 0.06966 },\n  { letter: 'Other', frequency: 0.55602 },\n]\n```\n\nThe same primitives cover labels, center content, padding, rounded corners,\nand concentric rings. See the catalog examples for a\n[labeled pie](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/93-labeled-pie/),\n[center-content donut](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/94-center-donut/),\n[rounded donut](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/95-rounded-donut/), and\n[nested donut](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/96-nested-donut/).\n\nRadial offsets are signed pixels applied after scale mapping. They do not\nchange the radius domain or reserve outer margin; leave space with\n`radiusRatio`, `inset`, or chart margins.\n\nSource order is the default. Use `orderBy` and `order` only for an explicit\nangular sort. Stable arc keys must come from the original row, not the\ngenerated slice index.\n\nEach allocated row keeps the original fields plus direct `source` and\n`sourceIndexes` lineage. Fixed allocation fields overwrite source fields with\nthe same names. `gapAngle` materializes direct empty space; the returned\n`padAngle: 0` prevents `radialArc` from padding that interval again.\n\n## Partial-circle gauge\n\nA gauge is the same composition over a restricted pie interval. It is not a\nseparate geometry implementation.\n\n```ts group=polar-partial-gauge env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { pie, polar, radialArc, radialText } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst value = Math.max(0, Math.min(100, 72))\nconst reading = { id: 'complete', value } as const\nconst parts = [reading, { id: 'remaining', value: 100 - value }] as const\nconst slices = pie(parts, {\n  value: 'value',\n  startAngle: -Math.PI * 0.75,\n  endAngle: Math.PI * 0.75,\n})\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      radiusRatio: 0.84,\n      scales: {\n        angle: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 1]) },\n        radius: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 1]) },\n      },\n\n      marks: [\n        radialArc(slices, {\n          innerRadius: ({ radius }) => radius * 0.72,\n          cornerRadius: 999,\n          color: 'id',\n          key: 'id',\n        }),\n        radialText([reading], {\n          angle: 0,\n          radius: 0,\n          text: (row) => `${row.value}%`,\n          key: 'id',\n          fill: 'currentColor',\n          fontSize: 20,\n          fontWeight: 700,\n        }),\n      ],\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  color: {\n    domain: ['complete', 'remaining'],\n    range: ['#ef4444', '#e2e8f0'],\n  },\n})\n```\n\nBound the input before layout and expose the exact value outside the arc. Arc\nlength is useful for a compact status summary, not fine comparison. Add ticks,\na needle, and a center label only when they carry meaning; see the\n[needle gauge](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/98-needle-gauge/) for that\ncomposition.\n\n## Radar profile\n\nRadar combines an inferred angle factory and a fixed radius instance with\npolar guides and radial marks. TanStack supplies both responsive ranges. The\nnormalization remains visible in a separate source file because it determines\nthe meaning of every radius.\n\n```ts group=polar-radar env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport {\n  angleGrid,\n  polar,\n  radialArea,\n  radialGrid,\n  radialLine,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\nimport { curveLinearClosed } from 'd3-shape'\nimport { events } from './data'\nimport { profile } from './normalize'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      radiusRatio: 0.72,\n      scales: {\n        angle: { scale: scalePoint<string>().domain(events), wrap: true },\n        radius: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 1]) },\n      },\n\n      guides: [\n        radialGrid({\n          values: [0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1],\n          shape: 'polygon',\n        }),\n        angleGrid({ labels: true }),\n      ],\n      marks: [\n        radialArea(profile, {\n          angle: 'event',\n          radius: 'relativePerformance',\n          curve: curveLinearClosed,\n          fill: '#7c3aed',\n          fillOpacity: 0.22,\n        }),\n        radialLine(profile, {\n          angle: 'event',\n          radius: 'relativePerformance',\n          curve: curveLinearClosed,\n          stroke: '#8b5cf6',\n          strokeWidth: 2,\n        }),\n      ],\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=polar-radar file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport interface DecathlonRow {\n  Country: string\n  '100 Meters': number\n  'Long Jump': number\n  'High Jump': number\n  '100 Meter Hurdles': number\n}\n\nexport const decathlon: readonly DecathlonRow[] = [\n  {\n    Country: 'United States',\n    '100 Meters': 10.35,\n    'Long Jump': 7.96,\n    'High Jump': 2.05,\n    '100 Meter Hurdles': 13.61,\n  },\n  {\n    Country: 'Great Britain',\n    '100 Meters': 10.44,\n    'Long Jump': 7.74,\n    'High Jump': 2.11,\n    '100 Meter Hurdles': 13.75,\n  },\n  {\n    Country: 'Germany',\n    '100 Meters': 10.67,\n    'Long Jump': 7.62,\n    'High Jump': 2.08,\n    '100 Meter Hurdles': 14.02,\n  },\n  {\n    Country: 'France',\n    '100 Meters': 10.58,\n    'Long Jump': 7.81,\n    'High Jump': 1.99,\n    '100 Meter Hurdles': 13.88,\n  },\n]\n\nexport const events = [\n  '100 Meters',\n  'Long Jump',\n  'High Jump',\n  '100 Meter Hurdles',\n] as const\n\nexport type RadarEvent = (typeof events)[number]\n```\n\n```ts group=polar-radar file=/src/normalize.ts collapsed\nimport { normalize, select } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { fold } from '@tanstack/charts/transform/fold'\nimport { decathlon, events } from './data'\nimport type { RadarEvent } from './data'\n\nconst timedEvents = new Set<RadarEvent>(['100 Meters', '100 Meter Hurdles'])\nconst folded = fold(decathlon, {\n  fields: events,\n  as: { key: 'event', value: 'result' },\n})\nconst normalized = normalize(folded, {\n  by: 'event',\n  value: (datum) =>\n    timedEvents.has(datum.event) ? -datum.result : datum.result,\n  basis: 'extent',\n  as: 'relativePerformance',\n})\n\nexport const profile = select(normalized, {\n  by: 'event',\n  select: 'first',\n})\n```\n\nUse radar for a small, fixed set of compatible dimensions. Keep every domain\nand direction explicit, and do not rank profiles by apparent filled area. See\nthe [comparative radar](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/99-comparative-radar/)\nwhen multiple profiles are the point of the chart.\n\n## Numeric polar line\n\nLightweight linear scales map numeric angle and radius values without changing\nthe mark API. Here a visible transform maps observation dates to angles while\npreserving the source temperature field.\n\n```ts group=polar-line env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport {\n  angleGrid,\n  polar,\n  radialGrid,\n  radialLine,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { dayOfYearAngle, seattle2012 } from './weather'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      scales: {\n        angle: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 360]) },\n        radius: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([-10, 40]) },\n      },\n\n      guides: [\n        radialGrid({ values: [0, 10, 20, 30, 40] }),\n        angleGrid({ values: [0, 90, 180, 270], labels: false }),\n      ],\n      marks: [\n        radialLine(seattle2012, {\n          angle: dayOfYearAngle,\n          radius: 'temp_max',\n          stroke: '#0f766e',\n        }),\n      ],\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=polar-line file=/src/weather.ts collapsed\nexport interface WeatherRow {\n  location: string\n  date: Date\n  temp_max: number\n}\n\nconst weather: readonly WeatherRow[] = [\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-01-15'), temp_max: 8.3 },\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-03-15'), temp_max: 12.2 },\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-05-15'), temp_max: 18.9 },\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-07-15'), temp_max: 25.6 },\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-09-15'), temp_max: 21.1 },\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-11-15'), temp_max: 11.7 },\n]\n\nexport const seattle2012 = weather.filter(\n  (row) => row.location === 'Seattle' && row.date.getUTCFullYear() === 2012,\n)\n\nexport function dayOfYearAngle(row: WeatherRow) {\n  const year = row.date.getUTCFullYear()\n  const start = Date.UTC(year, 0, 1)\n  const end = Date.UTC(year + 1, 0, 1)\n  return ((row.date.getTime() - start) / (end - start)) * 360\n}\n```\n\nThe [full-year polar line](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/106-polar-line/)\nuses the same transform with the complete weather series.\n\n## Numeric polar scatter\n\nThe same coordinate accepts independent points. Wind direction and speed stay\nas explicit transforms over source `u` and `v` measurements.\n\n```ts group=polar-scatter env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { angleGrid, polar, radialDot, radialGrid } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { latitudeBand, windDirection, windSpeed } from './wind'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      scales: {\n        angle: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 360]) },\n        radius: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 13]) },\n      },\n\n      guides: [\n        radialGrid({ values: [3, 6, 9, 12] }),\n        angleGrid({ values: [0, 90, 180, 270], labels: false }),\n      ],\n      marks: [\n        radialDot(latitudeBand, {\n          angle: windDirection,\n          radius: windSpeed,\n          r: 4.5,\n          fill: '#e11d48',\n        }),\n      ],\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=polar-scatter file=/src/wind.ts collapsed\nexport interface WindRow {\n  latitude: number\n  u: number\n  v: number\n}\n\nconst wind: readonly WindRow[] = [\n  { latitude: 48.125, u: 4.2, v: 1.6 },\n  { latitude: 48.125, u: 2.1, v: 5.8 },\n  { latitude: 48.125, u: -3.4, v: 6.2 },\n  { latitude: 48.125, u: -5.1, v: -2.3 },\n  { latitude: 48.125, u: 1.8, v: -4.7 },\n]\n\nexport const latitudeBand = wind.filter((row) => row.latitude === 48.125)\n\nexport function windDirection(row: WindRow) {\n  return (Math.atan2(row.v, row.u) * (180 / Math.PI) + 360) % 360\n}\n\nexport function windSpeed(row: WindRow) {\n  return Math.hypot(row.u, row.v)\n}\n```\n\nThe [catalog polar scatter](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/107-polar-scatter/)\nuses a denser sample from the same latitude band.\n\n## Radial bars\n\nChoose the mark by the quantitative direction. A rose extends one bar through\nradius for each angle band. Concentric radial bars extend through angle for\neach radius band. Band padding controls categorical occupancy.\n\n```ts group=polar-radial-bars env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { polar, radialBarRadius } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { frequencies } from './data'\n\nconst letters = frequencies.map((row) => row.letter)\nconst maximum = Math.max(...frequencies.map((row) => row.frequency))\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      radiusRatio: 0.8,\n      scales: {\n        angle: { scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.12) },\n        radius: {\n          scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, maximum]),\n          range: [({ radius }) => radius * 0.3, ({ radius }) => radius],\n        },\n      },\n\n      marks: [\n        radialBarRadius(frequencies, {\n          angle: 'letter',\n          radius: 'frequency',\n          color: 'letter',\n          key: 'letter',\n        }),\n      ],\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  color: {\n    domain: letters,\n    range: ['#2563eb', '#7c3aed', '#db2777', '#ea580c', '#16a34a'],\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=polar-radial-bars file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport interface FrequencyRow {\n  letter: string\n  frequency: number\n}\n\nexport const frequencies: readonly FrequencyRow[] = [\n  { letter: 'E', frequency: 0.12702 },\n  { letter: 'T', frequency: 0.09056 },\n  { letter: 'A', frequency: 0.08167 },\n  { letter: 'O', frequency: 0.07507 },\n  { letter: 'I', frequency: 0.06966 },\n]\n```\n\nAn omitted radius baseline in `radialBarRadius` starts at the physical center;\nthe responsive radius range controls the quantitative endpoints. Supply\n`radius1` when both endpoints are semantic values. Signed radius data should\nuse `radius1: 0` so semantic zero maps through the scale.\n\nUse `radialBarAngle` when values should extend around the circle instead. See\nthe [concentric radial-bar example](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/100-radial-bars/).\n\n## Polar hierarchy\n\nThe optional `sunburst` mark accepts flat hierarchy rows and owns value\naggregation, partitioning, responsive rings, and sector geometry.\n\n```ts group=polar-sunburst env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { sunburst } from '@tanstack/charts/hierarchy/sunburst'\nimport { polar } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { rows } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      radiusRatio: 0.88,\n      startAngle: Math.PI / 2,\n      endAngle: Math.PI / 2 - Math.PI * 2,\n      marks: [\n        sunburst(rows, {\n          path: 'name',\n          delimiter: '.',\n          value: 'size',\n          innerRadius: ({ radius }) => radius * 0.14,\n          ringPadding: 2,\n          color: 'branchId',\n          stroke: '#fff',\n        }),\n      ],\n      scales: {\n        angle: null,\n        radius: null,\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  color: { range: ['#7c3aed', '#0ea5e9', '#14b8a6'] },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=polar-sunburst file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport interface PackageRow {\n  name: string\n  size: number | null\n}\n\nexport const rows: readonly PackageRow[] = [\n  { name: 'app', size: null },\n  { name: 'app.ui', size: null },\n  { name: 'app.ui.button', size: 8 },\n  { name: 'app.ui.dialog', size: 5 },\n  { name: 'app.data', size: null },\n  { name: 'app.data.cache', size: 6 },\n  { name: 'app.data.client', size: 11 },\n]\n```\n\nUse `nodeId` and `parentId` for explicit parent-reference rows. Responsive\n`innerRadius` and `outerRadius` callbacks receive the final polar radius;\n`ringPadding` remains a fixed pixel gap. Every `SunburstNode` retains its\ndirect row and source index, while `branchId` gives descendants the color of\ntheir first ancestor below the root. See the\n[Sunburst Mark reference](../reference/marks/sunburst.md) and the\n[full Flare hierarchy](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/101-sunburst/).\n\n## Coordinate and bundle boundary\n\n`polar()` is a positionless container mark. It resolves one center and radius,\ncopies configured angle/radius scales, paints guide backgrounds, child marks,\nthen guide foreground labels, and emits ordinary scene nodes and focus points.\nThe outer chart therefore omits both Cartesian axes.\n\nThe polar entry uses D3 arc and radial path generators internally. Application\nsource can use compact angle and radius scales or upgrade either one to\n`d3-scale`; curve factories and application-owned pie layout can come directly\nfrom `d3-shape`. See\n[Polar Marks](../reference/marks/polar.md) for the complete API and\n[Bundle Size and Performance](../guides/bundle-size-and-performance.md) for\nthe isolated consumer budgets.\n\n`radialArc` also accepts existing D3 pie DTOs as interoperability input; native\n`pie` is preferred when flat fields, transform lineage, and direct gap\nsemantics are wanted.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- Keep angle for cyclic order or part-to-whole intervals.\n- Use native `pie` output rather than reimplementing angle accumulation.\n- Let marks infer identity from source IDs or unique positions; supply a key\n  when neither is available.\n- Preserve original values for tooltips and accessible summaries.\n- Keep radar dimension domains, directions, and units explicit.\n- Verify labels around the full circumference at narrow widths.\n- Prefer aligned bars or dots when precise comparison is the primary task.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Polar and Radar Charts\ndescription: Build native pie, donut, gauge, radar, line, scatter, radial bar, rose, and sunburst charts through the opt-in polar coordinate entry.\n---\n\nPolar geometry is available only from `@tanstack/charts/polar`. The container\nowns responsive center, angle, and radius ranges. Its eager `pie` transform\nowns value allocation; granular D3 modules still own configured scales, curve\nfactories, and final arc/path geometry.\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  angleGrid,\n  pie,\n  polar,\n  radialArc,\n  radialArea,\n  radialDot,\n  radialGrid,\n  radialLine,\n  radialRule,\n  radialText,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\n```\n\nThe package root stays Cartesian-sized when this subpath is not imported.\n\n## Pie and donut\n\nUse `pie` to turn totals into flat source-linked angular intervals.\n`radialArc` renders the intervals. This example uses a responsive inner radius\nfor a donut; return `0` instead for a pie.\n\n```ts group=polar-pie-donut env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { pie, polar, radialArc } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { alphabet } from './data'\n\nconst slices = pie(alphabet, { value: 'frequency' })\nconst letters = alphabet.map((row) => row.letter)\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      inset: 8,\n      radiusRatio: 0.82,\n      marks: [\n        radialArc(slices, {\n          innerRadius: ({ radius }) => radius * 0.58,\n          cornerRadius: 4,\n          color: 'letter',\n          key: 'letter',\n        }),\n      ],\n      scales: {\n        angle: null,\n        radius: null,\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  color: {\n    domain: letters,\n    range: ['#0ea5e9', '#6366f1', '#a855f7', '#ec4899', '#f97316', '#94a3b8'],\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=polar-pie-donut file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport interface AlphabetRow {\n  letter: string\n  frequency: number\n}\n\nexport const alphabet: readonly AlphabetRow[] = [\n  { letter: 'E', frequency: 0.12702 },\n  { letter: 'T', frequency: 0.09056 },\n  { letter: 'A', frequency: 0.08167 },\n  { letter: 'O', frequency: 0.07507 },\n  { letter: 'I', frequency: 0.06966 },\n  { letter: 'Other', frequency: 0.55602 },\n]\n```\n\nThe same primitives cover labels, center content, padding, rounded corners,\nand concentric rings. See the catalog examples for a\n[labeled pie](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/93-labeled-pie/),\n[center-content donut](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/94-center-donut/),\n[rounded donut](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/95-rounded-donut/), and\n[nested donut](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/96-nested-donut/).\n\nRadial offsets are signed pixels applied after scale mapping. They do not\nchange the radius domain or reserve outer margin; leave space with\n`radiusRatio`, `inset`, or chart margins.\n\nSource order is the default. Use `orderBy` and `order` only for an explicit\nangular sort. Stable arc keys must come from the original row, not the\ngenerated slice index.\n\nEach allocated row keeps the original fields plus direct `source` and\n`sourceIndexes` lineage. Fixed allocation fields overwrite source fields with\nthe same names. `gapAngle` materializes direct empty space; the returned\n`padAngle: 0` prevents `radialArc` from padding that interval again.\n\n## Partial-circle gauge\n\nA gauge is the same composition over a restricted pie interval. It is not a\nseparate geometry implementation.\n\n```ts group=polar-partial-gauge env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { pie, polar, radialArc, radialText } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\n\nconst value = Math.max(0, Math.min(100, 72))\nconst reading = { id: 'complete', value } as const\nconst parts = [reading, { id: 'remaining', value: 100 - value }] as const\nconst slices = pie(parts, {\n  value: 'value',\n  startAngle: -Math.PI * 0.75,\n  endAngle: Math.PI * 0.75,\n})\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      radiusRatio: 0.84,\n      scales: {\n        angle: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 1]) },\n        radius: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 1]) },\n      },\n\n      marks: [\n        radialArc(slices, {\n          innerRadius: ({ radius }) => radius * 0.72,\n          cornerRadius: 999,\n          color: 'id',\n          key: 'id',\n        }),\n        radialText([reading], {\n          angle: 0,\n          radius: 0,\n          text: (row) => `${row.value}%`,\n          key: 'id',\n          fill: 'currentColor',\n          fontSize: 20,\n          fontWeight: 700,\n        }),\n      ],\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  color: {\n    domain: ['complete', 'remaining'],\n    range: ['#ef4444', '#e2e8f0'],\n  },\n})\n```\n\nBound the input before layout and expose the exact value outside the arc. Arc\nlength is useful for a compact status summary, not fine comparison. Add ticks,\na needle, and a center label only when they carry meaning; see the\n[needle gauge](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/98-needle-gauge/) for that\ncomposition.\n\n## Radar profile\n\nRadar combines an inferred angle factory and a fixed radius instance with\npolar guides and radial marks. TanStack supplies both responsive ranges. The\nnormalization remains visible in a separate source file because it determines\nthe meaning of every radius.\n\n```ts group=polar-radar env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport {\n  angleGrid,\n  polar,\n  radialArea,\n  radialGrid,\n  radialLine,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { scalePoint } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/point'\nimport { curveLinearClosed } from 'd3-shape'\nimport { events } from './data'\nimport { profile } from './normalize'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      radiusRatio: 0.72,\n      scales: {\n        angle: { scale: scalePoint<string>().domain(events), wrap: true },\n        radius: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 1]) },\n      },\n\n      guides: [\n        radialGrid({\n          values: [0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1],\n          shape: 'polygon',\n        }),\n        angleGrid({ labels: true }),\n      ],\n      marks: [\n        radialArea(profile, {\n          angle: 'event',\n          radius: 'relativePerformance',\n          curve: curveLinearClosed,\n          fill: '#7c3aed',\n          fillOpacity: 0.22,\n        }),\n        radialLine(profile, {\n          angle: 'event',\n          radius: 'relativePerformance',\n          curve: curveLinearClosed,\n          stroke: '#8b5cf6',\n          strokeWidth: 2,\n        }),\n      ],\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=polar-radar file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport interface DecathlonRow {\n  Country: string\n  '100 Meters': number\n  'Long Jump': number\n  'High Jump': number\n  '100 Meter Hurdles': number\n}\n\nexport const decathlon: readonly DecathlonRow[] = [\n  {\n    Country: 'United States',\n    '100 Meters': 10.35,\n    'Long Jump': 7.96,\n    'High Jump': 2.05,\n    '100 Meter Hurdles': 13.61,\n  },\n  {\n    Country: 'Great Britain',\n    '100 Meters': 10.44,\n    'Long Jump': 7.74,\n    'High Jump': 2.11,\n    '100 Meter Hurdles': 13.75,\n  },\n  {\n    Country: 'Germany',\n    '100 Meters': 10.67,\n    'Long Jump': 7.62,\n    'High Jump': 2.08,\n    '100 Meter Hurdles': 14.02,\n  },\n  {\n    Country: 'France',\n    '100 Meters': 10.58,\n    'Long Jump': 7.81,\n    'High Jump': 1.99,\n    '100 Meter Hurdles': 13.88,\n  },\n]\n\nexport const events = [\n  '100 Meters',\n  'Long Jump',\n  'High Jump',\n  '100 Meter Hurdles',\n] as const\n\nexport type RadarEvent = (typeof events)[number]\n```\n\n```ts group=polar-radar file=/src/normalize.ts collapsed\nimport { normalize, select } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { fold } from '@tanstack/charts/transform/fold'\nimport { decathlon, events } from './data'\nimport type { RadarEvent } from './data'\n\nconst timedEvents = new Set<RadarEvent>(['100 Meters', '100 Meter Hurdles'])\nconst folded = fold(decathlon, {\n  fields: events,\n  as: { key: 'event', value: 'result' },\n})\nconst normalized = normalize(folded, {\n  by: 'event',\n  value: (datum) =>\n    timedEvents.has(datum.event) ? -datum.result : datum.result,\n  basis: 'extent',\n  as: 'relativePerformance',\n})\n\nexport const profile = select(normalized, {\n  by: 'event',\n  select: 'first',\n})\n```\n\nUse radar for a small, fixed set of compatible dimensions. Keep every domain\nand direction explicit, and do not rank profiles by apparent filled area. See\nthe [comparative radar](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/99-comparative-radar/)\nwhen multiple profiles are the point of the chart.\n\n## Numeric polar line\n\nLightweight linear scales map numeric angle and radius values without changing\nthe mark API. Here a visible transform maps observation dates to angles while\npreserving the source temperature field.\n\n```ts group=polar-line env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport {\n  angleGrid,\n  polar,\n  radialGrid,\n  radialLine,\n} from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { dayOfYearAngle, seattle2012 } from './weather'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      scales: {\n        angle: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 360]) },\n        radius: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([-10, 40]) },\n      },\n\n      guides: [\n        radialGrid({ values: [0, 10, 20, 30, 40] }),\n        angleGrid({ values: [0, 90, 180, 270], labels: false }),\n      ],\n      marks: [\n        radialLine(seattle2012, {\n          angle: dayOfYearAngle,\n          radius: 'temp_max',\n          stroke: '#0f766e',\n        }),\n      ],\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=polar-line file=/src/weather.ts collapsed\nexport interface WeatherRow {\n  location: string\n  date: Date\n  temp_max: number\n}\n\nconst weather: readonly WeatherRow[] = [\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-01-15'), temp_max: 8.3 },\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-03-15'), temp_max: 12.2 },\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-05-15'), temp_max: 18.9 },\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-07-15'), temp_max: 25.6 },\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-09-15'), temp_max: 21.1 },\n  { location: 'Seattle', date: new Date('2012-11-15'), temp_max: 11.7 },\n]\n\nexport const seattle2012 = weather.filter(\n  (row) => row.location === 'Seattle' && row.date.getUTCFullYear() === 2012,\n)\n\nexport function dayOfYearAngle(row: WeatherRow) {\n  const year = row.date.getUTCFullYear()\n  const start = Date.UTC(year, 0, 1)\n  const end = Date.UTC(year + 1, 0, 1)\n  return ((row.date.getTime() - start) / (end - start)) * 360\n}\n```\n\nThe [full-year polar line](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/106-polar-line/)\nuses the same transform with the complete weather series.\n\n## Numeric polar scatter\n\nThe same coordinate accepts independent points. Wind direction and speed stay\nas explicit transforms over source `u` and `v` measurements.\n\n```ts group=polar-scatter env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { angleGrid, polar, radialDot, radialGrid } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { latitudeBand, windDirection, windSpeed } from './wind'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      scales: {\n        angle: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 360]) },\n        radius: { scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, 13]) },\n      },\n\n      guides: [\n        radialGrid({ values: [3, 6, 9, 12] }),\n        angleGrid({ values: [0, 90, 180, 270], labels: false }),\n      ],\n      marks: [\n        radialDot(latitudeBand, {\n          angle: windDirection,\n          radius: windSpeed,\n          r: 4.5,\n          fill: '#e11d48',\n        }),\n      ],\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=polar-scatter file=/src/wind.ts collapsed\nexport interface WindRow {\n  latitude: number\n  u: number\n  v: number\n}\n\nconst wind: readonly WindRow[] = [\n  { latitude: 48.125, u: 4.2, v: 1.6 },\n  { latitude: 48.125, u: 2.1, v: 5.8 },\n  { latitude: 48.125, u: -3.4, v: 6.2 },\n  { latitude: 48.125, u: -5.1, v: -2.3 },\n  { latitude: 48.125, u: 1.8, v: -4.7 },\n]\n\nexport const latitudeBand = wind.filter((row) => row.latitude === 48.125)\n\nexport function windDirection(row: WindRow) {\n  return (Math.atan2(row.v, row.u) * (180 / Math.PI) + 360) % 360\n}\n\nexport function windSpeed(row: WindRow) {\n  return Math.hypot(row.u, row.v)\n}\n```\n\nThe [catalog polar scatter](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/107-polar-scatter/)\nuses a denser sample from the same latitude band.\n\n## Radial bars\n\nChoose the mark by the quantitative direction. A rose extends one bar through\nradius for each angle band. Concentric radial bars extend through angle for\neach radius band. Band padding controls categorical occupancy.\n\n```ts group=polar-radial-bars env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { polar, radialBarRadius } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { scaleBand } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/band'\nimport { scaleLinear } from '@tanstack/charts/scales/linear'\nimport { frequencies } from './data'\n\nconst letters = frequencies.map((row) => row.letter)\nconst maximum = Math.max(...frequencies.map((row) => row.frequency))\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      radiusRatio: 0.8,\n      scales: {\n        angle: { scale: () => scaleBand<string>().padding(0.12) },\n        radius: {\n          scale: scaleLinear().domain([0, maximum]),\n          range: [({ radius }) => radius * 0.3, ({ radius }) => radius],\n        },\n      },\n\n      marks: [\n        radialBarRadius(frequencies, {\n          angle: 'letter',\n          radius: 'frequency',\n          color: 'letter',\n          key: 'letter',\n        }),\n      ],\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  color: {\n    domain: letters,\n    range: ['#2563eb', '#7c3aed', '#db2777', '#ea580c', '#16a34a'],\n  },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=polar-radial-bars file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport interface FrequencyRow {\n  letter: string\n  frequency: number\n}\n\nexport const frequencies: readonly FrequencyRow[] = [\n  { letter: 'E', frequency: 0.12702 },\n  { letter: 'T', frequency: 0.09056 },\n  { letter: 'A', frequency: 0.08167 },\n  { letter: 'O', frequency: 0.07507 },\n  { letter: 'I', frequency: 0.06966 },\n]\n```\n\nAn omitted radius baseline in `radialBarRadius` starts at the physical center;\nthe responsive radius range controls the quantitative endpoints. Supply\n`radius1` when both endpoints are semantic values. Signed radius data should\nuse `radius1: 0` so semantic zero maps through the scale.\n\nUse `radialBarAngle` when values should extend around the circle instead. See\nthe [concentric radial-bar example](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/100-radial-bars/).\n\n## Polar hierarchy\n\nThe optional `sunburst` mark accepts flat hierarchy rows and owns value\naggregation, partitioning, responsive rings, and sector geometry.\n\n```ts group=polar-sunburst env=charts file=/src/chart.ts entry\nimport { defineChart } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { sunburst } from '@tanstack/charts/hierarchy/sunburst'\nimport { polar } from '@tanstack/charts/polar'\nimport { rows } from './data'\n\nexport default defineChart({\n  marks: [\n    polar({\n      radiusRatio: 0.88,\n      startAngle: Math.PI / 2,\n      endAngle: Math.PI / 2 - Math.PI * 2,\n      marks: [\n        sunburst(rows, {\n          path: 'name',\n          delimiter: '.',\n          value: 'size',\n          innerRadius: ({ radius }) => radius * 0.14,\n          ringPadding: 2,\n          color: 'branchId',\n          stroke: '#fff',\n        }),\n      ],\n      scales: {\n        angle: null,\n        radius: null,\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n  scales: {\n    x: null,\n    y: null,\n  },\n  color: { range: ['#7c3aed', '#0ea5e9', '#14b8a6'] },\n})\n```\n\n```ts group=polar-sunburst file=/src/data.ts collapsed\nexport interface PackageRow {\n  name: string\n  size: number | null\n}\n\nexport const rows: readonly PackageRow[] = [\n  { name: 'app', size: null },\n  { name: 'app.ui', size: null },\n  { name: 'app.ui.button', size: 8 },\n  { name: 'app.ui.dialog', size: 5 },\n  { name: 'app.data', size: null },\n  { name: 'app.data.cache', size: 6 },\n  { name: 'app.data.client', size: 11 },\n]\n```\n\nUse `nodeId` and `parentId` for explicit parent-reference rows. Responsive\n`innerRadius` and `outerRadius` callbacks receive the final polar radius;\n`ringPadding` remains a fixed pixel gap. Every `SunburstNode` retains its\ndirect row and source index, while `branchId` gives descendants the color of\ntheir first ancestor below the root. See the\n[Sunburst Mark reference](../reference/marks/sunburst.md) and the\n[full Flare hierarchy](https://tanstack.com/charts/catalog/101-sunburst/).\n\n## Coordinate and bundle boundary\n\n`polar()` is a positionless container mark. It resolves one center and radius,\ncopies configured angle/radius scales, paints guide backgrounds, child marks,\nthen guide foreground labels, and emits ordinary scene nodes and focus points.\nThe outer chart therefore omits both Cartesian axes.\n\nThe polar entry uses D3 arc and radial path generators internally. Application\nsource can use compact angle and radius scales or upgrade either one to\n`d3-scale`; curve factories and application-owned pie layout can come directly\nfrom `d3-shape`. See\n[Polar Marks](../reference/marks/polar.md) for the complete API and\n[Bundle Size and Performance](../guides/bundle-size-and-performance.md) for\nthe isolated consumer budgets.\n\n`radialArc` also accepts existing D3 pie DTOs as interoperability input; native\n`pie` is preferred when flat fields, transform lineage, and direct gap\nsemantics are wanted.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- Keep angle for cyclic order or part-to-whole intervals.\n- Use native `pie` output rather than reimplementing angle accumulation.\n- Let marks infer identity from source IDs or unique positions; supply a key\n  when neither is available.\n- Preserve original values for tooltips and accessible summaries.\n- Keep radar dimension domains, directions, and units explicit.\n- Verify labels around the full circumference at narrow widths.\n- Prefer aligned bars or dots when precise comparison is the primary task.\n"}
